Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Gauge Eight Bends After the Strike
- Story & Context: The debut runs at 126 BPM. Naran begins with a low yatga fifth whose upper note is bent upward after half a beat, changing the interval into a suspended color while Imogen keeps the bass root fixed. Batsaikhan holds one vibraphone witness tone so the bend becomes unmistakable. Khulan replaces every fourth rim hit with a muted frame-drum center stroke. Eleanor enters with a dry bassoon fundamental after the second SHADOW appears. At the midpoint, Naran performs the same SOURCE pitch but bends downward instead of upward; the drum pattern stays unchanged, yet the entire harmonic mood shifts. The track ends with one unbent note, deliberately denying the expected SHADOW.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Close overhead view of a yatga string crossing a paper thickness-gauge chart marked 8, with one straight pitch line bending upward after the attack while a second reference line stays fixed. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “gauge eight,” “after the strike,” “hold the root,” and “shadow moves.”

Track 2: Frosted Panel Keeps the Original Note
- Story & Context: At 132 BPM, Imogen programs the EP’s most syncopated bruk pattern, with displaced kicks and a late clipped snare. Naran repeats one upper-register SOURCE pitch while changing only the depth of each post-attack bend. Some SHADOW notes barely move; others cross a semitone far enough to create temporary dissonance against Batsaikhan’s fixed vibraphone minor seventh. Khulan plays only in the spaces where hats disappear. Eleanor sustains one bassoon note through multiple bends, functioning as a calibration reference. In the final minute, the sub is removed entirely, leaving yatga, snare, vibraphone, and bassoon to carry the groove through harmonic motion alone.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Frosted glass inspection panel with a single printed reference line and several hand-drawn pitch curves bending away from it at different depths, yatga bridge and vibraphone bar in shallow focus. Spoken fragments limited to “original note,” “same attack,” “different pressure,” and “glass keeps the line.”

Track 3: Shadow Mark Moves Before Packing
- Story & Context: The closer runs at 122 BPM and begins with the sparsest rhythm on the EP. Naran plays widely spaced low yatga notes, each followed by a slow upward bend that lasts nearly a full beat. Imogen uses only kick, ghost snare, and short root sub. Batsaikhan bows one vibraphone bar rather than striking it, creating a continuous fixed pitch beside the moving yatga. Khulan contributes one frame-drum rim knock every two bars. Eleanor enters late with a descending bassoon third. In the final section, Naran progressively reduces the bend depth until SOURCE and SHADOW become almost indistinguishable. The final note is plucked, left completely unbent, and allowed to decay into warehouse room tone.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Wooden glass-packing crate beside a calibration sheet where a graphite shadow mark has shifted slightly away from its printed reference before being boxed, one yatga string crossing the page diagonally. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “before packing,” “shadow mark,” “less pressure,” and “leave the last one straight.”

